{"name":"Mudra","hex":"#b8d0da","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mudra","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several formal symbolic hand postures used in classical dance of India and in Hindu and Buddhist iconography.","Any of the formal body positions and postures used in yoga and meditation."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mudra"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mudra","blocks":[{"text":"A mudra is a symbolic or ritual gesture or pose in Hinduism, Jainism and Buddhism. While some mudras involve the entire body, most are performed with the hands and fingers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Shiva_as_the_Lord_of_Dance_LACMA.jpg/330px-Shiva_as_the_Lord_of_Dance_LACMA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mudra","wikidata":"Q208030"},{"title":"Mudra (music)","blocks":[{"text":"In Indian classical music, particularly Carnatic music, a mudra is a term woven into compositions that indicates the identity of the composer, a patron, the raga, tala, or style. A composer might use his own name or a pseudonym. Not all composers have mudras, and they do not necessarily relate to the composer's name."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mudra_(music)","wikidata":"Q282109"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/WLA_lacma_Buddha_Shakyamuni_ca_850_Gaya_District.jpg/500px-WLA_lacma_Buddha_Shakyamuni_ca_850_Gaya_District.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/WLA_lacma_Buddha_Shakyamuni_ca_850_Gaya_District.jpg/500px-WLA_lacma_Buddha_Shakyamuni_ca_850_Gaya_District.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Indian Buddha Shakyamuni statue making the bhūmisparśa or \"earth witness\" mudra, c. 850","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWLA_l